ents.", "reference": "c. 1603–1606, William Shakespeare, “The Tragedie of King Lear”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act III, scene vi]:"}, {"text": "The fashion of his countenance was altered.", "reference": "1611, The Holy Bible, […] (King James Version), London: […] Robert Barker, […], →OCLC, Luke 9:29:"}, {"text": "My word! Well, you should have my colorful fashions to thank.", "reference": "2020 December 25, Chris Van Dusen, “Diamond of the First Water”, in Bridgerton, season 1, episode 1, spoken by Lady Portia Featherington:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(dated) Polite, fashionable, or genteel life; social position; good breeding.", "tags": ["countable", "dated", "uncountable"], "examples": ["men of fashion"], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "verb", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈfæʃən/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[ˈfæʃən] ~ [ˈfæʃn̩]", "tags": []}], "forms": [{"word": "fashions", "tags": ["present", "singular", "third-person"]}, {"word": "fashioning", "tags": ["participle", "present"]}, {"word": "fashioned", "tags": ["participle", "past"]}, {"word": "fashioned", "tags": ["past"]}, {"word": "fascion", "tags": ["alternative", "obsolete"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "To make, build or construct, especially in a crude or improvised way.", "tags": [], "examples": ["1918, Edgar Rice Burroughs, The Land That Time Forgot Chapter IX\nI have three gourds which I fill with water and take back to my cave against the long nights. I have fashioned a spear and a bow and arrow, that I may conserve my ammunition, which is running low."], "quotes": [{"text": "[…] a device fashioned by arguments against that kind of prey.", "r